A three-step mechanism has been suggested for the formation of carbonyl chloride:
- Step I: \( \text{Cl}_2 \rightarrow 2\text{Cl} \) (fast, equilibrium)
- Step II: \( \text{Cl} + \text{CO} \rightarrow \text{COCl} \) (fast, equilibrium)
- Step III: \( \text{COCl} + \text{Cl}_2 \rightarrow \text{COCl}_2 + \text{Cl} \) (slow)

What is the molecularity of the rate-determining step (Step III)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The rate-determining step is the slowest step in a reaction mechanism. In this case, it is step 3:

$$ \text{CO}_2 + \text{Cl}_2 \rightarrow \text{COC}_2 + \{text{C}_1 $$

To determine the order of the reaction, we need to count the number of molecules that react in the rate-determining step. In this case, two molecules react (one $CO_2$ and one $C_2$).

* **Un Uniuni:** One molecule
* **Bimolecular:** Two molecules
* **Termolecular:** Three molecules

Since two molecules react in the rate-determining step, the rate of reaction is bimolecular.

Therefore, the molarity of the rate-limiting step (Step 3) is **bimolecular**.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

