","Let $z$ be a complex number such that $(3 + \sqrt{3}i) z = -2\sqrt{3}i$.
To find $z$, we divide both side by $3 + \sqrt{3}i$:
$$z = \frac{-2\sqrt{3}i}{3 + \sqrt{3}i}$$
To simplify this fraction, we multiply the numerator and denominator by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $3 - \sqrt{3}i$:
$$z = \frac{-2\sqrt{3}i}{3 + \sqrt{3}i} \cdot \frac{3 - \sqrt{3}i}{3 - \sqrt{3}i} = \frac{-2\sqrt{3}i(3 - \sqrt{3}i}}{(3 + \sqrt{3}i)(3 - \sqrt{3}i)}$$
$$z = \frac{-6\sqrt{3}i + 2\sqrt{3}\cdot \sqrt{3}i^2}{9 - (3\sqrt{3}i)^2} = \frac{-6\sqrt{3}i - 6}{9 - 9i^2} = \frac{-6 - 6\sqrt{3}i}{9 + 9} = \frac{-6 - 6\sqrt{3}i}{18}$$
$$z = \frac{-6}{18} - \frac{6\sqrt{3}}{18}i = -\frac{1}{3} - \frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}i$$
The complex number $z$ is $z = -\frac{1}{3} - \frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}i$.
The complex conjugate of $z$ is $z^* = -\frac{1}{3} + \frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}i$.
The point corresponding to $z'$ is $\left(-\frac{1}{3}, \frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}\right)$.
Since the real part is negative and the imaginary part is positive, the point is in the second quadrant.

