Find the value of \( \lim_{t \to \infty} I(t) \), where \( I(t) = \left[(1/3)\sin t + \cos t\right] e^{-t/2} + 4 \).<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","Let $I(t) = \left[(1/3)\sin t + \cos t\right] e^{-t/2} + 4$.
We want to find $\lim_{t \to \infty} I(t)$.
We have
$$ \lim_{t \to \infty} I(t) = \lim_{t \infty} \left[ \left(\frac{1}{3} \sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t/2} + 4 \right] $$
Since $e^{-t/2} \to 0$ as $t \to \infty$, we have
$$ \lim_{t \to \infty} \left[ \left(\frac{11}{3} \sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t/2} \right] = 0 $$
Since $\sin t$ and $\cos t$ oscillate between $-1$ and $1$, we have
$$ \left| \frac{1}{3} \sin t + \cos t \right| \le \left| \frac{1}{3} \sin t \right| + \left| \cos t \right| \le \frac{1}{3} + 1 = \frac{4}{3} $$
Thus,
$$ \left| \left(\frac{1}{3} \sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t/2} \right| \le \frac{4}{3} e^{-t/2} $$
As $t \to \infty$, $e^{-t/2} \to 0$, so $\left[ \left(\frac{1}{3} \sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t2} \right] \to 0$.
Therefore,
$$ \lim_{t \to \infty} I(t) = \lim_{t \infty} \left[ \left(\frac{1}{3} \sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t/2} + 4 \right] = 0 + 4 = 4 $$

We can use the limit limit-as-limit rule.
Let $I(t) = \left[ \left(\frac{1}{3} \sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t/2} \right] + 4$.
We have
$$ \lim_{t \to \infty} \left[ \left(\frac{1}{3} \sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t/2} \right] = \lim_{t \to \infty} \left(\frac{1}{3} \sin t + \cos t\right) \lim_{t \to \infty} e^{-t/2} $$
Since $\lim_{t \to \infty} e^{-t/2} = 0$, we have
$$ \lim_{t \to \infty} \left[ \left(\frac{1}{3} \sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t/2} \right] = \left(\frac{1}{3} \sin t + \cos t\right) \cdot 0 = 0 $$
Therefore,
$$ \lim_{t \infty} I(t) = 0 + 4 = 4 $$

