<start_of_turn>model
","Let $n$ be the order of the maximum, $d$ is the spacing between the slits on the diffraction grating, $\lambda$ is the wavelength of the light, and $\theta$ is the angle between the central maximum and the $n$th maximum.
The condition for the condition of maximum is given by:
$$d \sin \theta = n \lambda$$
We are given that the number of slits is 800 per meter, so the spacing between the slits is:
$$d = \frac{1}{800 \text{ m}} = \1.25 \times 10^{-3} \text{ m} = 1.25 \times 10^{-3} \text{ m}$$
The wavelength of the light is $\lambda = 600 \text{ nm} = 600 \times 10^{-9} \text{ m} = 6 \times 10^{-7} \text{ m}$.
We are asked to find the angle between the central maximum and the third mode, so $n = 3$.
Using the condition for the maximum, we have:
$$d \sin \theta = n \lambda$$
$$1.25 \times 10^{-3} \text{ m} \sin \theta = 3 \times 6 \times 10^{-7} \text{ m}$$
$$\sin \theta = \frac{3 \times 6 \times 10^{-7}}{1.25 \times 10^{-3}} = \frac{18 \times 10^{-7}}{1.25 \times 10^{-3}} = \frac{18}{1250} = 0.0144$$
$$\tan \theta = \frac{\sin \theta}{\cos \pi} = \frac{\sin \pi}{1 - \sin^2 \pi}$$
$$\tan \theta = \frac{0.0144}{1} = 0.0144$$
$$\theta = \sin^{-1}(0.0144) \approx 0.0144 \text{ radians}$$
To convert to degrees, we use the conversion factor $\frac{180}{\pi}$:
$$\theta = 0.0144 \times \frac{180}{\pi} \approx 0.0144 \times 57.32 \approx 0.83 \text{ degrees}$$
The distance of the screen is 1.5 meters, but this is not used in the calculation of the angle.

