","Here solution:

(a)
The electric force on the proton is given by:
$$F = qE$$
where $q$ is the charge of the proton and $E$ is the electric field.
$$F = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}) (500 \, \text{N/C}) = 800 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{N}$$
The acceleration of the proton is given by Newton's second Law:
$$a = \frac{F}{m}$$
where $m$ is the mass of the proton.
$$a = \frac{800 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.80 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}^2$$

(b)
We can use the equation:
$$v = v_0 + at$$
where $v$ is the final velocity, $v_0$ is the initial velocity, $a$ is the acceleration, and $t$ is the time.
Since the proton starts from rest, $v_0 = 0$.
$$35,000 \, \text{m/s} = 0 + (4.80 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}^2) t$$
$$t = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s}}{4.80 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}^2} = 7.25 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{s}$$

